Mr. Technical here again,
Ok here's the scoop on burn-ins. 

Back in the 70's and even into the early 80's poeple had given their kids  black and white TV's for their rooms. These screens were highly sussceptable  to burning images on the phosphor.  with the frequency of color sets increasing, this problem was greatly reduced. It still is possible, but it does take a LONG time to burn-in.
The routine, called PIZBRK (pizza break) in the DNA manual, has a preset time.
I can't remember the exact length, but if no key is pressed, and the interrupts are enabled, it is exactly like clockwork. It uses the clock and a countdown timer. Hitting any key or joystick function will stop this. It works like your screen saver in Windows,  except you cannot change the duration of time on the Arcade without doing a special machine code program.
Ken

